\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A normal link that changes a transverse character}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take two layers and a transverse circle of period #{4}.
The transverse links are trivial, the fiber is a line, and the
normal link at #{x} is #{U_s(x,1)=(-1)^x}. It takes a constant
character to the alternating one. At #{aM=1}, for
#{0\ne v\in\im P_-}, put #{\psi_1(x)=(-1)^xv/2} on the bottom
and #{\psi_2(x)=v} on the top. The on-site factor in the
[slab operator](fdip-01AE) before the grading and division
by #{a} is #{2} on the alternating character and #{0} on the
constant character. The forward normal hop cancels #{2\psi_1};
the backward hop vanishes since #{P_+v=0}. Thus the full field
is null. Its top component on its own has a nonzero hop to
the bottom. The null field cannot be written as a sum of null
fields each supported on a single face.}
